===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|grammar}} The infinitive mood or mode (a [[grammatical mood]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1847|author=J. J. P. Le Brethon; L. Sandier|title=Guide to the French language; especially devised for persons who wish to study that language without the assistance of a teacher. the tenth edition, revised and corrected|location=London|page=69
|passage=The MANNERS of acting, in grammar called ''modes'' or ''moods'', are four; '''''Infinitive''''', ''Imperative'', ''Indicative'', ''Subjunctive'' or ''Conjunctive''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1857|author=Henry Tindall|title=A grammar and vocabulary of the Namaqua-Hottentot language|page=38
|passage=There are four moods, the '''Infinitive''', Imperative, Indicative, and Subjunctive. [...] the '''Infinitive''' is used to express a thing in a general manner.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} A non-[[finite]] verb form considered [[neutral]] with respect to inflection; depending on language variously found used with [[auxiliary verb]]s, in [[subordinate clause]]s, or acting as a [[gerund]], and often as the [[dictionary form]].
# {{lb|en|grammar}} A [[verbal noun]] formed from the infinitive of a verb.

